    "question": {
      ":question/id": "baa2d38c-e020-5a26-8e9f-29e2e246186c",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $D$ given below?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $D$ is a singular matrix\n- **II.** $det(D)=2$\n- **III.** $D$ is **not** a singular matrix\n\n$$\nD=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(D) & =|\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-3)|\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}|+(-1)|\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}|+4|\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-3)(-1+0)+(-1)(-3-8)+4(0-2) \\\\  & =(-3)\\cdot (-1)+(-1)\\cdot (-11)+4\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =3+11-8 \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(D)=6\\ne 0$, the matrix is **not** singular. \n\nTherefore, only statement III is true.",
